g-blocks-found-in-bennu-samples)\n  62. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2025NatAs...9..199G)\n  63. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-024-02472-9)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glycine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct ten distinct reaction-sequence pathways: four cyanide-derived variants and six experimentally distinguished reductive-amination variants. These are not ten unrelated mechanisms. The network exceeds the minimum node counts to represent intermediates, consumed reductants, and competing sinks explicitly. HCN, formaldehyde, glyoxylate, ammonia, nitrate, hydroxylamine, hydrogen, and reduced minerals are declared boundary feedstocks; their geological supply is not assumed to be solved. In particular, no efficient CO2-to-glyoxylate synthesis is asserted. Neutral formulas represent acid-base pools: glyoxylic and glycolic acids are predominantly carboxylates under alkaline conditions, ammonia includes ammonium, and the nitric-acid node represents supplied nitrate rather than addition of strong acid. Starting species have null environment_formed; other entries give one representative formation environment, not an exclusive location. Shared molecule nodes identify chemical identity without requiring incompatible experimental conditions to coexist. Ferroan-brucite oxidation is expressed through idealized FeOOH and Mg(OH)2 endmember balances, not a claim that these are the exclusively observed mineral products. Terminal coproducts use the allowed intermediate role. All pathways are anoxic; surface photodestruction, mineral adsorption, hydroxy-acid competition, and hydrolysis bottlenecks remain limitations.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanimine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldimine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH3N/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoethanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h2,4H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoethanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H6N2O/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H2,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h3H,1H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(carbamoylamino)acetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydantoic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-iminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeH2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) hydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferrous hydroxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeHO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(III) oxide hydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferric oxyhydroxide, phase unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeMg2H6O6\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) dimagnesium hexahydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferroan brucite, idealized Fe:Mg = 1:2 composition\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"MgH2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"magnesium hydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"brucite\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"HNO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"nitric acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"nitric acid, representing the nitrate pool\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/HNO3/c2-1(3)4/h(H,2,3,4)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(hydroxyimino)acetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylic acid oxime\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_024\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"native iron\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_025\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-[(carboxymethyl)amino]acetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"iminodiacetic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible formaldehyde-ammonia condensation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic concentrated pool or porewater; approximately pH 8-9; ammonia must be available alongside formaldehyde and cyanide. Methanimine is transient, not an accumulated pure feedstock.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to formaldehyde through a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to methanimine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the conventional mechanistic entry to formaldehyde Strecker chemistry underlying discharge-derived glycine synthesis (Miller, 1953; Johnson et al., 2008). The overall route is experimentally established, but aqueous methanimine formation alone is unfavorable and requires downstream trapping. Simultaneous feedstock supply and competing formaldehyde reactions remain unresolved geological constraints.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide trapping of methanimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 8-9; sufficient dissolved cyanide and ammonia; HCN/CN- speciation is implicit. Excessive evaporation can lose volatile feedstocks.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, yielding aminoacetonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminoacetonitrile is the established Strecker precursor to glycine and is also used in cyanide-based prebiotic networks (Miller, 1953; Patel et al., 2015). Ammonia availability determines competition with glycolonitrile formation; no mineral catalyst is required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetonitrile hydration to glycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Aqueous hydron or hydroxide in separate acidic or alkaline hydrolysis settings\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"general acid or base catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Warm wet stage after nitrile formation; acidic or alkaline conditions accelerate hydrolysis. Long residence may be necessary; no quantitative completion time is inferred from the supplied research.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water addition to the nitrile, assisted by proton transfer, gives the primary amide glycinamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile-to-amide-to-amino-acid hydrolysis is the accepted Strecker sequence. The supplied research supports the overall conversion but does not provide validated rates for these proposed surface conditions (Miller, 1953; Hennet et al., 1992, summarized by Andersson and Holm, 2000). This is a kinetic bottleneck rather than an assumed rapid ambient reaction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Aqueous hydron or hydroxide in separate acidic or alkaline hydrolysis settings\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"general acid or base catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Sustained warm aqueous residence; appreciable acid or base accelerates otherwise slow amide hydrolysis. Ammonia product will largely be ammonium in acidic solutions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the amide C-N bond produces glycine and ammonia/ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This completes the standard Strecker hydrolysis sequence associated with experimentally observed glycine production (Miller, 1953; Hennet et al., 1992, summarized by Andersson and Holm, 2000). Neither near-neutral surface hydrolysis rates nor natural acid/base cycling are assumed sufficient without further evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 7-9; reversible reaction in anoxic cyanide-containing pools. Formation competes with methanimine trapping.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the formaldehyde carbonyl, followed by protonation of the alkoxide to form glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolonitrile is an experimentally grounded branch point in cyanide-derived prebiotic chemistry (Patel et al., 2015). Its formation needs neither UV nor copper; those agents belong to upstream photoredox homologation, which is not claimed by this reaction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven conversion of glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich solution, approximately pH 8-10; sufficient residence for cyanohydrin-imine equilibration. Conditions must favor the amino rather than hydroxy branch.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanohydrin dissociation and carbonyl-imine exchange allow re-trapping as aminoacetonitrile; the net reaction replaces hydroxyl by amino.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The glycolonitrile-to-aminoacetonitrile branch is described in the cyanosulfidic network (Patel et al., 2015). This net step should not be interpreted as facile direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group. Ammonia supply and sequential reagent delivery remain limiting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing glycolonitrile hydrolysis to glycolate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Aqueous hydron or hydroxide\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"general acid or base catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Warm aqueous hydrolysis; especially consequential when ammonia-dependent conversion to aminoacetonitrile is inefficient.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lumped nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis convert glycolonitrile to glycolic acid and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the chemically expected hydroxy-acid sink for the cyanohydrin branch described alongside cyanide-derived amino-acid chemistry (Patel et al., 2015). The supplied review identifies this competition, but supplies no step-specific kinetic constants; glycolate production must not be counted as glycine yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-dependent trapping of aminoacetonitrile as hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-313 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm, with retained dissolved inorganic carbon\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonium-carbonate/bicarbonate-containing concentrated solution; approximately pH 8-10. Formation rate and yield for this exact glycine precursor are not established by the supplied report.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminonitrile carbamylation by CO2 is followed by cyclization and rearrangement through Bucherer-Bergs-type intermediates to unsubstituted hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin-mediated amino-acid synthesis is supported by the supplied literature (Ferris et al., 1974; Pulletikurti et al., 2022). However, the latter study concerns ketoacid substrates and does not by itself validate this exact formaldehyde-derived substrate at these conditions. The low-temperature implementation here is an explicitly extrapolative branch of established Bucherer-Bergs chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin ring opening to hydantoic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Aqueous hydron in acidic geothermal fluid\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 MPa, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"general acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic pressurized geothermal fracture fluid; prolonged residence may be necessary. This temperature window is a proposed operating range, not a measured optimum from the supplied papers.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic opening of the cyclic ureide yields N-carbamoylglycine, also called hydantoic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis is a supported amino-acid-release mechanism (Ferris et al., 1974; Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). Exact kinetics and geological acid availability are unresolved here. The approximately 353 K hydrolysis reported for related ketoacid-derived intermediates must not automatically be assigned to unsubstituted hydantoin (Pulletikurti et al., 2022).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoic acid hydrolysis and decarbamylation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Aqueous hydron in acidic geothermal fluid\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 MPa\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"general acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continuation of acidic wet hydrolysis followed by cooling; avoid prolonged residence at temperatures where amino-acid destruction dominates.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the carbamoyl group releases glycine; the remaining carbamic-acid equivalent decomposes to ammonia and CO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the standard chemical completion of hydantoin-to-amino-acid hydrolysis, consistent with glycine release from hydrolyzable cyanide-derived material (Ferris et al., 1974; Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). The detailed geochemical implementation is conditional. Thermal destruction limits extrapolation toward hot black-smoker conditions (Bada et al., 1995; Aubrey et al., 2009).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite assemblage as an experimental redox buffer, not a demonstrated Strecker catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 10 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN, formaldehyde, and ammonium supplied to an anoxic reactor; CO2/H2 gas phase approximately 3:1. Intermediate formation and subsequent hydrolysis occur in the same experimental setting.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lumped imine formation and cyanide addition produce transient aminoacetonitrile under pressurized aqueous conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The overall hydrothermal Strecker route was demonstrated by Hennet et al. (1992), as summarized by Andersson and Holm (2000). The reaction-node split is mechanistic bookkeeping, not a claim that aminoacetonitrile was isolated in that experiment. Concentrated supplied precursors and the specific redox-buffer assemblage limit geological generalization.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite assemblage as an experimental redox buffer\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 10 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Lumped hydration and amide hydrolysis over the experimental 25-54 hour interval; subsequent cooling is required for accumulation outside the reactor.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminoacetonitrile hydrolyzes through glycinamide to glycine, releasing its nitrile nitrogen as ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycine concentrations of approximately 9.3-11.6 mM are reported for the overall experiment in a secondary summary of Hennet et al. (1992), not independently verified here from the primary paper (Andersson and Holm, 2000). This supports a moderate-temperature pressurized route, not sustained synthesis above 473 K; high-temperature destruction is a major limitation (Bada et al., 1995; Aubrey et al., 2009).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible glyoxylate-ammonia condensation to glycine imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"293-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1-5 bar, depending on the downstream reduction\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 7-10; imine formation occurs in the same solution as downstream reduction. Glyoxylate hydration and imine hydrolysis are competing equilibria.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to glyoxylate through a hemiaminal; reversible dehydration generates glycine imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycine imine and its instability in water were directly investigated by Paczelt et al. (2023). This node represents a transient mechanistic intermediate, not a requirement to isolate or transport it. The lack of an established efficient prebiotic glyoxylate source remains an upstream limitation for every branch using it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed hydrogenation of glycine imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ni(0) supported on silica-alumina, the experimentally tested catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 8; reported ammonium concentrations span 6-150 mM. The imine is formed in situ from glyoxylate and ammonia; direct glyoxylate hydrogenation competes.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nickel activates H2 and transfers hydrogen to the imine C=N bond, producing glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The overall reductive amination is supported by Kaur et al. (2024), including activity at substantially lower ammonium concentrations than many earlier mineral experiments. The supported catalyst is synthetic: comparable activity of naturally dispersed nickel on silicates is a geological extrapolation. Reported combined amino- and hydroxy-acid yields must not be treated as glycine-only yields.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteorite-metal-mediated hydrogenation of glycine imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ground iron-meteorite metal containing reactive Fe-Ni phases\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"experimentally tested iron-meteorite metal powder\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 8; ammonium-containing solution; reactivity depends on meteorite composition, fresh surface area, corrosion, and passivation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reactive meteoritic metal surfaces promote hydrogen-dependent reduction of the in situ imine to glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ground iron meteorites were tested alongside supported metal catalysts by Kaur et al. (2024). This is a separate experimentally distinguished catalyst pathway, not a new carbon-skeleton mechanism or evidence that all meteorites perform equally. Natural hydrogen retention and accessible metal surface area remain uncertain.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Mixed-valence iron-hydroxide reduction of glycine imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide assemblage, approximately 1:1 initially; Fe(II) is a consumed electron donor\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"mixed-valence iron-hydroxide interface; reducing capacity is not catalytic\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 7-10; benchmark experiments used 2.5 mM glyoxylate and 375 mM NH4Cl. Lower-ammonium tests were also reported, but no unsupported glycine yield is assigned to them.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Interfacial electron transfer reduces the imine while Fe(II) is oxidized to Fe(III). Ferrous hydroxide and ferric oxyhydroxide express an idealized endmember redox balance.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The relevant glyoxylate-to-glycine experiments are Barge et al. (2020), not the pyruvate-focused Barge et al. (2019) study. Mixed-valence composition matters: this node does not assert that pure Fe(OH)2 has the same selectivity. Glycolate formation competes, and the exact spent mineral phases are not resolved by this simplified balance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Ferroan-brucite reduction of ammonia-derived glycine imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ferroan brucite with approximately one-third Fe(II) occupancy; stoichiometric reductant and reactive interface\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"293 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Initial pH approximately 10; benchmark starting solutions contained 100 mM glyoxylate and 100 mM NH4Cl; experiments extended to six weeks. Available Fe(II), not merely substrate concentration, limits reduction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-bound Fe(II) supplies the electrons needed to reduce the in situ imine. FeOOH and Mg(OH)2 are formal oxidized endmembers for atom and electron accounting.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ammonium-dependent glycine synthesis on ferroan brucite was demonstrated by Chimiak et al. (2024). No percentage yield is transferred without its limiting-reagent definition. The organic transformation is experimentally supported, whereas complete separation into the displayed mineral endmembers is an explicitly idealized phase balance. Independent replication and product availability remain open.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Coupled nitrate reduction and glyoxylate amination on ferroan brucite\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ferroan brucite as a consumed Fe(II) electron donor and nitrogen-reduction interface\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"293 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Initial pH approximately 10; benchmark experiment supplied 100 mM glyoxylate and 100 mM nitrate as a salt; six-week timescale. The HNO3 node is a neutral bookkeeping form of nitrate, not the actual alkaline species.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lumped nitrate reduction generates reduced nitrogen that is incorporated into glyoxylate-derived glycine. The net transformation requires more reducing equivalents than ammonia-based amination; mineral oxidation is represented by idealized endmembers.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This coupled route was demonstrated by Chimiak et al. (2024), with lower yields than the ammonium system. Up to 97% of glycine in nitrate experiments was mineral-associated and detected after acid digestion, so total detected product is not equivalent to freely available glycine. The exact nitrogen-intermediate sequence and oxidized mineral phases are not asserted. Nitrate supply under anoxia remains a separate boundary condition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-hydroxylamine condensation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"approximately 343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic glyoxylate/hydroxylamine solution used with metallic iron in the downstream step; the supplied report does not establish a step-specific optimum pH or oxime yield.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine condenses with the glyoxylate carbonyl to form an oxime, releasing water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycine formation from glyoxylate using hydroxylamine and metallic iron is supported by Muchowska et al. (2019). Resolving that overall transformation through the standard oxime intermediate is a mechanistic inference, not a claim that the supplied study separately quantified this step. A sustained prebiotic hydroxylamine source is not established by this network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron reduction of glyoxylic acid oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Native Fe(0), a stoichiometrically consumed reductant and reactive surface\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"approximately 343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Fresh metallic-iron surface; anoxic water; passivation and competing corrosion can limit organic reduction. Fe(OH)2 is an idealized Fe(II) corrosion-product balance.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Iron oxidation supplies electrons for oxime C=N reduction and N-O bond cleavage, yielding glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The overall hydroxylamine/Fe(0)-dependent production of glycine is experimentally supported by Muchowska et al. (2019). The isolated oxime-reduction node is a mechanistic decomposition, and the displayed ferrous hydroxide is formal product accounting rather than an exclusively identified corrosion phase. Iron is consumed, not mislabeled as a indefinitely regenerated catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing nickel-catalyzed glyoxylate hydrogenation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ni(0) supported on silica-alumina\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 8; competes in the same solution as reductive amination. Relative rates depend on ammonium concentration and catalyst selectivity.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogenation of the glyoxylate carbonyl produces glycolate instead of reducing the nitrogen-containing imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) report substantial hydroxy-acid coproduct formation with nickel and meteoritic catalysts. This explicit sink prevents combined amino/hydroxy-acid yields from being misrepresented as glycine yields.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_022\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing iron-hydroxide reduction of glyoxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Fe(II)-bearing iron-hydroxide assemblage; consumed reducing equivalents\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"iron-hydroxide interface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 7-10; operates alongside the mixed-valence mineral amination branch. Fe(II)/Fe(III) ratio and ammonium concentration control competition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Electron transfer reduces the glyoxylate carbonyl to glycolate while Fe(II) is oxidized.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolate competes directly with glycine in the glyoxylate experiments of Barge et al. (2020). The Fe(OH)2-to-FeOOH representation is a formal endmember balance, not a claim that every experimental precipitate has these pure phases.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_023\",\n      \"name\": \"Secondary reductive amination trapping glycine as iminodiacetate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ferroan brucite as a consumed Fe(II) reductant\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"293 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Initially alkaline, approximately pH 10; prolonged contact with excess glyoxylate after glycine begins accumulating.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glycine's amino group condenses with another glyoxylate molecule; reduction of the resulting imine gives iminodiacetate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Iminodiacetic acid was observed among ferroan-brucite reaction products by Chimiak et al. (2024). The displayed sequential reductive-amination mechanism is a chemically motivated interpretation of that product, not an independently resolved kinetic sequence. This is a glycine-loss branch, and its mineral products remain idealized redox endmembers.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_024\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing ferroan-brucite reduction of glyoxylate to glycolate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ferroan brucite as a consumed Fe(II) reductant\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"293 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Initial pH approximately 10; competes with both ammonium- and nitrate-dependent glycine formation and consumes the same finite mineral reducing capacity.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-derived reducing equivalents convert the glyoxylate carbonyl to a hydroxyl group without nitrogen incorporation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolate is a reported coproduct in the ferroan-brucite experiments (Chimiak et al., 2024). Explicitly including it is necessary for realistic electron-budget and selectivity estimates. The organic product is supported experimentally; the displayed oxidized mineral endmembers are bookkeeping approximations.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface imine-first Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde and ammonia form transient methanimine, cyanide traps it as aminoacetonitrile, and staged aqueous hydrolysis yields glycine. Feedstock concentration and slow hydrolysis are the main constraints.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-entry Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde is initially trapped as glycolonitrile. Ammonia-dependent equilibration redirects this pool to aminoacetonitrile before hydrolysis. This is the glycine-relevant cyanohydrin branch, not a claim to reconstruct the entire upstream cyanosulfidic photoredox system.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-trapped hydantoin pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"An aminoacetonitrile precursor is trapped through Bucherer-Bergs-type chemistry as hydantoin, then hydrolyzed through hydantoic acid to glycine. The exact low-temperature formation conditions and geochemical hydrolysis implementation are extrapolative, explicitly lower-confidence portions of this pathway.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Pressurized hydrothermal Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde, ammonia, and HCN react and hydrolyze in the same approximately 423 K pressurized aqueous setting. This experimentally distinguished operating pathway shares Strecker chemistry with P1 but does not require a surface-to-vent transfer.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Supported-nickel and hydrogen reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied glyoxylate condenses with ammonia and undergoes nickel-catalyzed imine hydrogenation at approximately 295 K and 5 bar H2. Geological glyoxylate supply and natural equivalents of the supported catalyst remain unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteoritic-metal and hydrogen reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"The same transient glyoxylate-derived imine is reduced using hydrogen and experimentally tested iron-meteorite metal. This is a distinct catalyst realization, not an independent carbon-skeleton mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Mixed-valence iron-hydroxide reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate-ammonia condensation is followed by reduction at a mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide interface near 343 K. Fe(II) is consumed, and competing glycolate formation reduces glycine selectivity.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonium-fed ferroan-brucite reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Mineral-bound Fe(II) in ferroan brucite reduces an ammonia-derived glycine imine near 293 K. The route is limited by accessible mineral reducing equivalents, long reaction times, and side-product formation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Nitrate-fed ferroan-brucite glycine synthesis\",\n      \"description\": \"A coupled mineral reaction reduces externally supplied nitrate and incorporates nitrogen into glyoxylate-derived glycine. This pathway has a distinct nitrogen feedstock and a larger electron requirement than ammonium-fed amination; much of the detected product can remain mineral-associated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_018\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxylamine and native-iron oxime-reduction pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate condenses with hydroxylamine, followed by native-iron-mediated reduction to glycine. The overall transformation is experimentally supported, while the explicit oxime-node decomposition is mechanistic inference and hydroxylamine supply remains conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_019\", \"rxn_020\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_023\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_015\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_020\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_023\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_021\", \"rxn_022\", \"rxn_024\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_021\",\n        \"rxn_022\",\n        \"rxn_023\",\n        \"rxn_024\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glycine", "overall_strategy": "Construct ten distinct reaction-sequence pathways: four cyanide-derived variants and six experimentally distinguished reductive-amination variants. These are not ten unrelated mechanisms. The network exceeds the minimum node counts to represent intermediates, consumed reductants, and competing sinks explicitly. HCN, formaldehyde, glyoxylate, ammonia, nitrate, hydroxylamine, hydrogen, and reduced minerals are declared boundary feedstocks; their geological supply is not assumed to be solved. In particular, no efficient CO2-to-glyoxylate synthesis is asserted. Neutral formulas represent acid-base pools: glyoxylic and glycolic acids are predominantly carboxylates under alkaline conditions, ammonia includes ammonium, and the nitric-acid node represents supplied nitrate rather than addition of strong acid. Starting species have null environment_formed; other entries give one representative formation environment, not an exclusive location. Shared molecule nodes identify chemical identity without requiring incompatible experimental conditions to coexist. Ferroan-brucite oxidation is expressed through idealized FeOOH and Mg(OH)2 endmember balances, not a claim that these are the exclusively observed mineral products. Terminal coproducts use the allowed intermediate role. All pathways are anoxic; surface photodestruction, mineral adsorption, hydroxy-acid competition, and hydrolysis bottlenecks remain limitations.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CH3N", "iupac_name": "methanimine", "common_name": "formaldimine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH3N/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoethanenitrile", "common_name": "aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h2,4H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-aminoethanamide", "common_name": "glycinamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H6N2O/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H2,4,5)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C2H3NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanenitrile", "common_name": "glycolonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C2H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyacetic acid", "common_name": "glycolic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h3H,1H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H4N2O2", "iupac_name": "imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H6N2O3", "iupac_name": "2-(carbamoylamino)acetic acid", "common_name": "hydantoic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C2H3NO2", "iupac_name": "2-iminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine imine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "FeH2O2", "iupac_name": "iron(II) hydroxide", "common_name": "ferrous hydroxide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "FeHO2", "iupac_name": "iron(III) oxide hydroxide", "common_name": "ferric oxyhydroxide, phase unspecified", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "FeMg2H6O6", "iupac_name": "iron(II) dimagnesium hexahydroxide", "common_name": "ferroan brucite, idealized Fe:Mg = 1:2 composition", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "MgH2O2", "iupac_name": "magnesium hydroxide", "common_name": "brucite", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "HNO3", "iupac_name": "nitric acid", "common_name": "nitric acid, representing the nitrate pool", "inchi": "InChI=1S/HNO3/c2-1(3)4/h(H,2,3,4)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C2H3NO3", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)acetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "native iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_025", "formula": "C4H7NO4", "iupac_name": "2-[(carboxymethyl)amino]acetic acid", "common_name": "iminodiacetic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Reversible formaldehyde-ammonia condensation", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic concentrated pool or porewater; approximately pH 8-9; ammonia must be available alongside formaldehyde and cyanide. Methanimine is transient, not an accumulated pure feedstock."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to formaldehyde through a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to methanimine.", "reasoning": "This is the conventional mechanistic entry to formaldehyde Strecker chemistry underlying discharge-derived glycine synthesis (Miller, 1953; Johnson et al., 2008). The overall route is experimentally established, but aqueous methanimine formation alone is unfavorable and requires downstream trapping. Simultaneous feedstock supply and competing formaldehyde reactions remain unresolved geological constraints."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanide trapping of methanimine", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8-9; sufficient dissolved cyanide and ammonia; HCN/CN- speciation is implicit. Excessive evaporation can lose volatile feedstocks."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, yielding aminoacetonitrile.", "reasoning": "Aminoacetonitrile is the established Strecker precursor to glycine and is also used in cyanide-based prebiotic networks (Miller, 1953; Patel et al., 2015). Ammonia availability determines competition with glycolonitrile formation; no mineral catalyst is required."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Aminoacetonitrile hydration to glycinamide", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous hydron or hydroxide in separate acidic or alkaline hydrolysis settings"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "general acid or base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Warm wet stage after nitrile formation; acidic or alkaline conditions accelerate hydrolysis. Long residence may be necessary; no quantitative completion time is inferred from the supplied research."}, "mechanism": "Water addition to the nitrile, assisted by proton transfer, gives the primary amide glycinamide.", "reasoning": "Nitrile-to-amide-to-amino-acid hydrolysis is the accepted Strecker sequence. The supplied research supports the overall conversion but does not provide validated rates for these proposed surface conditions (Miller, 1953; Hennet et al., 1992, summarized by Andersson and Holm, 2000). This is a kinetic bottleneck rather than an assumed rapid ambient reaction."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous hydron or hydroxide in separate acidic or alkaline hydrolysis settings"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "general acid or base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Sustained warm aqueous residence; appreciable acid or base accelerates otherwise slow amide hydrolysis. Ammonia product will largely be ammonium in acidic solutions."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the amide C-N bond produces glycine and ammonia/ammonium.", "reasoning": "This completes the standard Strecker hydrolysis sequence associated with experimentally observed glycine production (Miller, 1953; Hennet et al., 1992, summarized by Andersson and Holm, 2000). Neither near-neutral surface hydrolysis rates nor natural acid/base cycling are assumed sufficient without further evidence."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 7-9; reversible reaction in anoxic cyanide-containing pools. Formation competes with methanimine trapping."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the formaldehyde carbonyl, followed by protonation of the alkoxide to form glycolonitrile.", "reasoning": "Glycolonitrile is an experimentally grounded branch point in cyanide-derived prebiotic chemistry (Patel et al., 2015). Its formation needs neither UV nor copper; those agents belong to upstream photoredox homologation, which is not claimed by this reaction."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Ammonia-driven conversion of glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich solution, approximately pH 8-10; sufficient residence for cyanohydrin-imine equilibration. Conditions must favor the amino rather than hydroxy branch."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanohydrin dissociation and carbonyl-imine exchange allow re-trapping as aminoacetonitrile; the net reaction replaces hydroxyl by amino.", "reasoning": "The glycolonitrile-to-aminoacetonitrile branch is described in the cyanosulfidic network (Patel et al., 2015). This net step should not be interpreted as facile direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group. Ammonia supply and sequential reagent delivery remain limiting."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Competing glycolonitrile hydrolysis to glycolate", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous hydron or hydroxide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "general acid or base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Warm aqueous hydrolysis; especially consequential when ammonia-dependent conversion to aminoacetonitrile is inefficient."}, "mechanism": "Lumped nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis convert glycolonitrile to glycolic acid and ammonia.", "reasoning": "This is the chemically expected hydroxy-acid sink for the cyanohydrin branch described alongside cyanide-derived amino-acid chemistry (Patel et al., 2015). The supplied review identifies this competition, but supplies no step-specific kinetic constants; glycolate production must not be counted as glycine yield."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "CO2-dependent trapping of aminoacetonitrile as hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-313 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm, with retained dissolved inorganic carbon", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonium-carbonate/bicarbonate-containing concentrated solution; approximately pH 8-10. Formation rate and yield for this exact glycine precursor are not established by the supplied report."}, "mechanism": "Aminonitrile carbamylation by CO2 is followed by cyclization and rearrangement through Bucherer-Bergs-type intermediates to unsubstituted hydantoin.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin-mediated amino-acid synthesis is supported by the supplied literature (Ferris et al., 1974; Pulletikurti et al., 2022). However, the latter study concerns ketoacid substrates and does not by itself validate this exact formaldehyde-derived substrate at these conditions. The low-temperature implementation here is an explicitly extrapolative branch of established Bucherer-Bergs chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydantoin ring opening to hydantoic acid", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Aqueous hydron in acidic geothermal fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393-423 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 MPa, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "general acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic pressurized geothermal fracture fluid; prolonged residence may be necessary. This temperature window is a proposed operating range, not a measured optimum from the supplied papers."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic opening of the cyclic ureide yields N-carbamoylglycine, also called hydantoic acid.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin hydrolysis is a supported amino-acid-release mechanism (Ferris et al., 1974; Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). Exact kinetics and geological acid availability are unresolved here. The approximately 353 K hydrolysis reported for related ketoacid-derived intermediates must not automatically be assigned to unsubstituted hydantoin (Pulletikurti et al., 2022)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydantoic acid hydrolysis and decarbamylation", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003", "mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Aqueous hydron in acidic geothermal fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393-423 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 MPa", "catalyst": "general acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Continuation of acidic wet hydrolysis followed by cooling; avoid prolonged residence at temperatures where amino-acid destruction dominates."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of the carbamoyl group releases glycine; the remaining carbamic-acid equivalent decomposes to ammonia and CO2.", "reasoning": "This is the standard chemical completion of hydantoin-to-amino-acid hydrolysis, consistent with glycine release from hydrolyzable cyanide-derived material (Ferris et al., 1974; Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). The detailed geochemical implementation is conditional. Thermal destruction limits extrapolation toward hot black-smoker conditions (Bada et al., 1995; Aubrey et al., 2009)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydrothermal Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite assemblage as an experimental redox buffer, not a demonstrated Strecker catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "423 K", "pressure": "approximately 10 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN, formaldehyde, and ammonium supplied to an anoxic reactor; CO2/H2 gas phase approximately 3:1. Intermediate formation and subsequent hydrolysis occur in the same experimental setting."}, "mechanism": "Lumped imine formation and cyanide addition produce transient aminoacetonitrile under pressurized aqueous conditions.", "reasoning": "The overall hydrothermal Strecker route was demonstrated by Hennet et al. (1992), as summarized by Andersson and Holm (2000). The reaction-node split is mechanistic bookkeeping, not a claim that aminoacetonitrile was isolated in that experiment. Concentrated supplied precursors and the specific redox-buffer assemblage limit geological generalization."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Hydrothermal aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite assemblage as an experimental redox buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "423 K", "pressure": "approximately 10 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Lumped hydration and amide hydrolysis over the experimental 25-54 hour interval; subsequent cooling is required for accumulation outside the reactor."}, "mechanism": "Aminoacetonitrile hydrolyzes through glycinamide to glycine, releasing its nitrile nitrogen as ammonia.", "reasoning": "Glycine concentrations of approximately 9.3-11.6 mM are reported for the overall experiment in a secondary summary of Hennet et al. (1992), not independently verified here from the primary paper (Andersson and Holm, 2000). This supports a moderate-temperature pressurized route, not sustained synthesis above 473 K; high-temperature destruction is a major limitation (Bada et al., 1995; Aubrey et al., 2009)."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Reversible glyoxylate-ammonia condensation to glycine imine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "293-343 K", "pressure": "approximately 1-5 bar, depending on the downstream reduction", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 7-10; imine formation occurs in the same solution as downstream reduction. Glyoxylate hydration and imine hydrolysis are competing equilibria."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to glyoxylate through a hemiaminal; reversible dehydration generates glycine imine.", "reasoning": "Glycine imine and its instability in water were directly investigated by Paczelt et al. (2023). This node represents a transient mechanistic intermediate, not a requirement to isolate or transport it. The lack of an established efficient prebiotic glyoxylate source remains an upstream limitation for every branch using it."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed hydrogenation of glycine imine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni(0) supported on silica-alumina, the experimentally tested catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8; reported ammonium concentrations span 6-150 mM. The imine is formed in situ from glyoxylate and ammonia; direct glyoxylate hydrogenation competes."}, "mechanism": "Nickel activates H2 and transfers hydrogen to the imine C=N bond, producing glycine.", "reasoning": "The overall reductive amination is supported by Kaur et al. (2024), including activity at substantially lower ammonium concentrations than many earlier mineral experiments. The supported catalyst is synthetic: comparable activity of naturally dispersed nickel on silicates is a geological extrapolation. Reported combined amino- and hydroxy-acid yields must not be treated as glycine-only yields."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Meteorite-metal-mediated hydrogenation of glycine imine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ground iron-meteorite metal containing reactive Fe-Ni phases"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "experimentally tested iron-meteorite metal powder", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8; ammonium-containing solution; reactivity depends on meteorite composition, fresh surface area, corrosion, and passivation."}, "mechanism": "Reactive meteoritic metal surfaces promote hydrogen-dependent reduction of the in situ imine to glycine.", "reasoning": "Ground iron meteorites were tested alongside supported metal catalysts by Kaur et al. (2024). This is a separate experimentally distinguished catalyst pathway, not a new carbon-skeleton mechanism or evidence that all meteorites perform equally. Natural hydrogen retention and accessible metal surface area remain uncertain."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Mixed-valence iron-hydroxide reduction of glycine imine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide assemblage, approximately 1:1 initially; Fe(II) is a consumed electron donor"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "mixed-valence iron-hydroxide interface; reducing capacity is not catalytic", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 7-10; benchmark experiments used 2.5 mM glyoxylate and 375 mM NH4Cl. Lower-ammonium tests were also reported, but no unsupported glycine yield is assigned to them."}, "mechanism": "Interfacial electron transfer reduces the imine while Fe(II) is oxidized to Fe(III). Ferrous hydroxide and ferric oxyhydroxide express an idealized endmember redox balance.", "reasoning": "The relevant glyoxylate-to-glycine experiments are Barge et al. (2020), not the pyruvate-focused Barge et al. (2019) study. Mixed-valence composition matters: this node does not assert that pure Fe(OH)2 has the same selectivity. Glycolate formation competes, and the exact spent mineral phases are not resolved by this simplified balance."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Ferroan-brucite reduction of ammonia-derived glycine imine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_018", "mol_020"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ferroan brucite with approximately one-third Fe(II) occupancy; stoichiometric reductant and reactive interface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "293 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Initial pH approximately 10; benchmark starting solutions contained 100 mM glyoxylate and 100 mM NH4Cl; experiments extended to six weeks. Available Fe(II), not merely substrate concentration, limits reduction."}, "mechanism": "Mineral-bound Fe(II) supplies the electrons needed to reduce the in situ imine. FeOOH and Mg(OH)2 are formal oxidized endmembers for atom and electron accounting.", "reasoning": "Ammonium-dependent glycine synthesis on ferroan brucite was demonstrated by Chimiak et al. (2024). No percentage yield is transferred without its limiting-reagent definition. The organic transformation is experimentally supported, whereas complete separation into the displayed mineral endmembers is an explicitly idealized phase balance. Independent replication and product availability remain open."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Coupled nitrate reduction and glyoxylate amination on ferroan brucite", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_021", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_018", "mol_020", "mol_004"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ferroan brucite as a consumed Fe(II) electron donor and nitrogen-reduction interface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "293 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Initial pH approximately 10; benchmark experiment supplied 100 mM glyoxylate and 100 mM nitrate as a salt; six-week timescale. The HNO3 node is a neutral bookkeeping form of nitrate, not the actual alkaline species."}, "mechanism": "Lumped nitrate reduction generates reduced nitrogen that is incorporated into glyoxylate-derived glycine. The net transformation requires more reducing equivalents than ammonia-based amination; mineral oxidation is represented by idealized endmembers.", "reasoning": "This coupled route was demonstrated by Chimiak et al. (2024), with lower yields than the ammonium system. Up to 97% of glycine in nitrate experiments was mineral-associated and detected after acid digestion, so total detected product is not equivalent to freely available glycine. The exact nitrogen-intermediate sequence and oxidized mineral phases are not asserted. Nitrate supply under anoxia remains a separate boundary condition."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Glyoxylate-hydroxylamine condensation", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_004"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "approximately 343 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic glyoxylate/hydroxylamine solution used with metallic iron in the downstream step; the supplied report does not establish a step-specific optimum pH or oxime yield."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine condenses with the glyoxylate carbonyl to form an oxime, releasing water.", "reasoning": "Glycine formation from glyoxylate using hydroxylamine and metallic iron is supported by Muchowska et al. (2019). Resolving that overall transformation through the standard oxime intermediate is a mechanistic inference, not a claim that the supplied study separately quantified this step. A sustained prebiotic hydroxylamine source is not established by this network."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "Native-iron reduction of glyoxylic acid oxime", "inputs": ["mol_023", "mol_024", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_017"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Native Fe(0), a stoichiometrically consumed reductant and reactive surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "approximately 343 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Fresh metallic-iron surface; anoxic water; passivation and competing corrosion can limit organic reduction. Fe(OH)2 is an idealized Fe(II) corrosion-product balance."}, "mechanism": "Iron oxidation supplies electrons for oxime C=N reduction and N-O bond cleavage, yielding glycine.", "reasoning": "The overall hydroxylamine/Fe(0)-dependent production of glycine is experimentally supported by Muchowska et al. (2019). The isolated oxime-reduction node is a mechanistic decomposition, and the displayed ferrous hydroxide is formal product accounting rather than an exclusively identified corrosion phase. Iron is consumed, not mislabeled as a indefinitely regenerated catalyst."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Competing nickel-catalyzed glyoxylate hydrogenation", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_016"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni(0) supported on silica-alumina"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8; competes in the same solution as reductive amination. Relative rates depend on ammonium concentration and catalyst selectivity."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogenation of the glyoxylate carbonyl produces glycolate instead of reducing the nitrogen-containing imine.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) report substantial hydroxy-acid coproduct formation with nickel and meteoritic catalysts. This explicit sink prevents combined amino/hydroxy-acid yields from being misrepresented as glycine yields."}, {"id": "rxn_022", "name": "Competing iron-hydroxide reduction of glyoxylate", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fe(II)-bearing iron-hydroxide assemblage; consumed reducing equivalents"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "iron-hydroxide interface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 7-10; operates alongside the mixed-valence mineral amination branch. Fe(II)/Fe(III) ratio and ammonium concentration control competition."}, "mechanism": "Electron transfer reduces the glyoxylate carbonyl to glycolate while Fe(II) is oxidized.", "reasoning": "Glycolate competes directly with glycine in the glyoxylate experiments of Barge et al. (2020). The Fe(OH)2-to-FeOOH representation is a formal endmember balance, not a claim that every experimental precipitate has these pure phases."}, {"id": "rxn_023", "name": "Secondary reductive amination trapping glycine as iminodiacetate", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_014", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_025", "mol_018", "mol_020", "mol_004"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ferroan brucite as a consumed Fe(II) reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "293 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Initially alkaline, approximately pH 10; prolonged contact with excess glyoxylate after glycine begins accumulating."}, "mechanism": "Glycine's amino group condenses with another glyoxylate molecule; reduction of the resulting imine gives iminodiacetate.", "reasoning": "Iminodiacetic acid was observed among ferroan-brucite reaction products by Chimiak et al. (2024). The displayed sequential reductive-amination mechanism is a chemically motivated interpretation of that product, not an independently resolved kinetic sequence. This is a glycine-loss branch, and its mineral products remain idealized redox endmembers."}, {"id": "rxn_024", "name": "Competing ferroan-brucite reduction of glyoxylate to glycolate", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_018", "mol_020"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ferroan brucite as a consumed Fe(II) reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "293 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Initial pH approximately 10; competes with both ammonium- and nitrate-dependent glycine formation and consumes the same finite mineral reducing capacity."}, "mechanism": "Mineral-derived reducing equivalents convert the glyoxylate carbonyl to a hydroxyl group without nitrogen incorporation.", "reasoning": "Glycolate is a reported coproduct in the ferroan-brucite experiments (Chimiak et al., 2024). Explicitly including it is necessary for realistic electron-budget and selectivity estimates. The organic product is supported experimentally; the displayed oxidized mineral endmembers are bookkeeping approximations."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Surface imine-first Strecker pathway", "description": "Formaldehyde and ammonia form transient methanimine, cyanide traps it as aminoacetonitrile, and staged aqueous hydrolysis yields glycine. Feedstock concentration and slow hydrolysis are the main constraints.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Cyanohydrin-entry Strecker pathway", "description": "Formaldehyde is initially trapped as glycolonitrile. Ammonia-dependent equilibration redirects this pool to aminoacetonitrile before hydrolysis. This is the glycine-relevant cyanohydrin branch, not a claim to reconstruct the entire upstream cyanosulfidic photoredox system.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_006", "mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "CO2-trapped hydantoin pathway", "description": "An aminoacetonitrile precursor is trapped through Bucherer-Bergs-type chemistry as hydantoin, then hydrolyzed through hydantoic acid to glycine. The exact low-temperature formation conditions and geochemical hydrolysis implementation are extrapolative, explicitly lower-confidence portions of this pathway.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Pressurized hydrothermal Strecker pathway", "description": "Formaldehyde, ammonia, and HCN react and hydrolyze in the same approximately 423 K pressurized aqueous setting. This experimentally distinguished operating pathway shares Strecker chemistry with P1 but does not require a surface-to-vent transfer.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Supported-nickel and hydrogen reductive amination", "description": "Supplied glyoxylate condenses with ammonia and undergoes nickel-catalyzed imine hydrogenation at approximately 295 K and 5 bar H2. Geological glyoxylate supply and natural equivalents of the supported catalyst remain unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_013", "rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Meteoritic-metal and hydrogen reductive amination", "description": "The same transient glyoxylate-derived imine is reduced using hydrogen and experimentally tested iron-meteorite metal. This is a distinct catalyst realization, not an independent carbon-skeleton mechanism.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_013", "rxn_015"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Mixed-valence iron-hydroxide reductive amination", "description": "Glyoxylate-ammonia condensation is followed by reduction at a mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide interface near 343 K. Fe(II) is consumed, and competing glycolate formation reduces glycine selectivity.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_013", "rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Ammonium-fed ferroan-brucite reductive amination", "description": "Mineral-bound Fe(II) in ferroan brucite reduces an ammonia-derived glycine imine near 293 K. The route is limited by accessible mineral reducing equivalents, long reaction times, and side-product formation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_013", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Nitrate-fed ferroan-brucite glycine synthesis", "description": "A coupled mineral reaction reduces externally supplied nitrate and incorporates nitrogen into glyoxylate-derived glycine. This pathway has a distinct nitrogen feedstock and a larger electron requirement than ammonium-fed amination; much of the detected product can remain mineral-associated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_018"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Hydroxylamine and native-iron oxime-reduction pathway", "description": "Glyoxylate condenses with hydroxylamine, followed by native-iron-mediated reduction to glycine. The overall transformation is experimentally supported, while the explicit oxime-node decomposition is mechanistic inference and hydroxylamine supply remains conditional.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_019", "rxn_020"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_023"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_014", "mol_015"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_006", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_011"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_012"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_010", "rxn_012", "rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_017", "rxn_018", "rxn_020"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_023"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_009", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_007", "rxn_021", "rx