m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Glycine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct ten distinct reaction-sequence pathways: four cyanide-derived variants and six experimentally distinguished reductive-amination variants. These are not ten unrelated mechanisms. The network exceeds the minimum node counts to represent intermediates, consumed reductants, and competing sinks explicitly. HCN, formaldehyde, glyoxylate, ammonia, nitrate, hydroxylamine, hydrogen, and reduced minerals are declared boundary feedstocks; their geological supply is not assumed to be solved. In particular, no efficient CO2-to-glyoxylate synthesis is asserted. Neutral formulas represent acid-base pools: glyoxylic and glycolic acids are predominantly carboxylates under alkaline conditions, ammonia includes ammonium, and the nitric-acid node represents supplied nitrate rather than addition of strong acid. Starting species have null environment_formed; other entries give one representative formation environment, not an exclusive location. Shared molecule nodes identify chemical identity without requiring incompatible experimental conditions to coexist. Ferroan-brucite oxidation is expressed through idealized FeOOH and Mg(OH)2 endmember balances, not a claim that these are the exclusively observed mineral products. Terminal