itions for unsubstituted hydantoin ring opening and hydantoic-acid cleavage, and the physical handoff to the heated compartment, remain unspecified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Provide substrate-specific evidence and pH, temperature and duration for hydantoin formation and both hydrolysis stages.\",\n        \"Describe transport or local heating of the hydantoin-containing solution, including dilution, retention and subsequent cooling.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.92,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.95,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.7,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.94,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.88,\n      \"feedback\": \"The two net reactions form a valid hydrothermal Strecker route. The supplied account of Hennet et al. (1992) supports glycine production near 150 degrees Celsius and 10 bar, although its quantitative results are reported through a secondary source. The Hydrothermal label alone does not specify a productive temperature, pressure or residence time; it also does not imply destructive temperatures above 200 degrees Celsius.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify an experimentally supported temperature-pressure window, residence time, gas composition and redox-buffer inventory.\",\n        \"Encode two waters in rxn_012 and describe cooling or removal before prolonged high-temperature degradation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.9