y and glycolate production before predicting glycine accumulation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.97,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.94\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.75,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.95,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.88,\n      \"feedback\": \"Cyanohydrin formation followed by ammonia-dependent aminonitrile formation is consistent with the supplied Strecker and Patel et al. (2015) discussion. rxn_004 is credible as a net conversion through aqueous equilibria, without requiring an elementary displacement of hydroxide. Upstream photoredox chemistry is unnecessary at the declared feedstock boundary. The explicit glycolate branch, rxn_019, correctly captures a selectivity loss, but its competition with productive conversion is unquantified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Parameterize aminonitrile versus cyanohydrin partitioning using ammonia concentration, pH, temperature and reaction time.\",\n        \"Provide hydrolysis conditions and rates for both the productive nitrile branch and competing rxn_019.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.97,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.81\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.87,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.91,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.64,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.88,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.82,\n      \"feedback\": \"The five-step sequence is inferred as imine-first aminonitrile formation followed by rxn_007\u2013rxn_009. Bucherer\u2013Bergs capture and eventual hydantoin hydrolysis are chemically credible. However, Pulletikurti et al. (2022) studied alpha-ketoacid-derived chemistry; its reported operating conditions do not directly establish hydrolysis rates for this unsubstituted hydantoin/N-carbamoylglycine sequence. A Surface label alone does not establish an effective hydrolytic release regime.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Attach explicit reaction-ID sequences to pathways and cite substrate-specific evidence for aminoacetonitrile-to-hydantoin conversion.\",\n        \"Establish pH, temperature and residence-time requirements for both hydrolysis steps rather than transferring mild conditions from differently substituted hydantoins.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.94\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.94,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.96,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.69,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.96,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.88,\n      \"feedback\": \"The lumped reaction is a valid representation of hydrothermal Strecker synthesis, and ammonia appearing on both sides does not invalidate it. The supplied Hennet et al. (1992) evidence supports glycine formation around 150 degrees C and 10 bar, although the quantitative results are reported through Andersson and Holm (2000). Those numerical conditions are not actually attached to rxn_010 in the submitted network.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify temperature, pressure, gas composition, ammonia concentration and residence time, with a citation distinguishing primary from secondary evidence.\",\n        \"Describe the pyrite\u2013pyrrhotite\u2013magnetite assemblage as an experimental redox buffer rather than a demonstrated essential catalyst, and avoid unsupported extrapolation to hotter systems.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.93,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.62,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.96,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.86,\n      \"feedback\": \"Kaur et al. (2024, Chem) strongly supports the overall hydrogen-driven reductive amination chemistry. Glycine imine should remain an equilibrating mechanistic intermediate, consistent with Paczelt et al. (2023), rather than an assumed stable stock. Environmental confidence is lower because the supported nickel material and hydrogen confinement are not defined for the stated environment. Competing rxn_020 also requires an active hydrogenation catalyst; molecular hydrogen alone is insufficient under the cited mild conditions.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_020\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Attach the experimental anchor of approximately 22 degrees C, pH 8 and 5 bar hydrogen, with catalyst support, loading and reaction time.\",\n        \"Identify a physically available active nickel material and explicitly assign its catalytic role to both rxn_012 and rxn_020; keep synthetic supported nickel distinct from an untested natural analogue.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.92,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.69,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.95,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.88,\n      \"feedback\": \"The meteorite-mediated variant is supported by the supplied Kaur et al. (2024) evidence and is legitimately distinct as an experimental catalyst variant, not a new reaction family. Its implementation remains conditional on accessible active metal surfaces, adequate hydrogen availability and limited passivation. The shared glycolate reaction is chemically appropriate when assigned to the same active metal catalyst.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_020\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify meteorite composition, particle size, exposed metal fraction and the relevant experimental catalyst preparation.\",\n        \"Define hydrogen pressure or dissolved concentration, confinement and passivation timescales, and measure amino-acid versus hydroxy-acid selectivity.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.94\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.95,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.88,\n      \"feedback\": \"Barge et al. (2020, JGR Planets) directly supports glyoxylate-to-glycine chemistry on mixed-valence iron hydroxides. The declared endmember representation and initial ferric boundary inventory are acceptable abstractions and do not require oxygen generation within the network. Fe(II) supplies reducing equivalents rather than acting as an indefinitely reusable catalyst. The actual mineral composition, ferric fraction and operating conditions remain unspecified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify mineral preparation, Fe(II)/Fe(III) ratio, ammonia concentration, pH and temperature using the glycine-specific 2020 study.\",\n        \"Separate the initial ferric inventory from