ons reduce nitrate nitrogen to the amine oxidation state and two reduce the carbonyl/imine functionality. rxn_016 should be interpreted as an empirical net reaction, not an elementary mechanism. Low reported yields limit flux confidence, while mineral association does not defeat the explicitly defined chemical-identity target. Formal proton accounting does not itself imply an acidic environment.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify nitrate and glyoxylate concentrations, mineral loading, pH evolution and reaction duration, with electron-limited maximum yields.\",\n        \"Distinguish dissolved glycine from mineral-associated, acid-extractable glycine without equating analytical digestion with environmental desorption.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.92,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.93,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.7,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.94,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.87,\n      \"feedback\": \"Oxime formation followed by native-iron reduction is chemically plausible and consistent with the hydroxylamine/iron experiments of Muchowska et al. (2019). The supplied summary supports the overall transformation more directly than it establishes every discrete intermediate. The balance audit accepts rxn_018; its remaining issues concern operating conditions, iron corrosion/passivation and the separately flagged mol_020 identity metadata. Hydroxylamine supply is an acknowledged boundary assumption, not an omitted mandatory synthesis step.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_018\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Normalize mol_020 to one iron atom with charge +2, for example using the structural representation [Fe+2], and reconcile its formula and InChI.\",\n        \"Specify pH, temperature, iron surface preparation and reagent concentrations, and distinguish direct oxime evidence from mechanistic inference.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.87,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"This is a credible, connected glycine-synthesis network at its declared feedstock boundary, but it is not approved at the 0.90 network threshold. All 25 molecule nodes participate, IDs are used consistently in the reaction list, and complete productive routes reach mol_015. Accepting the deterministic audit as instructed, all 22 reaction sets are balanced or balanceable and none is classified as a conservation violation. The mol_020 formula/structure discrepancy requires metadata repair, not rejection of rxn_018. Neutral acid-base pools, mineral endmember equivalents and the initial ferric boundary inventory are reasonable declared abstractions. The supplied literature supports the principal chemistry, and the network appropriately acknowledges upstream supply limitations, competing glycolate production, glycine consumption, passivation and mineral binding. No penalty is assigned for the selected feedstocks, shared mechanisms, pathway count or environment count. The main limitation is operational specification: despite the strategy referring to experimental anchors and screening windows, the submitted reaction entries contain no numerical conditions, reaction-level citations or defined physical habitats beyond broad labels. In particular, Biochemical does not specify a geochemical environment. Hydrolysis kinetics, hydantoin release conditions, active catalyst availability and hydrogen confinement therefore remain insufficiently established. Each productive route can operate within its assigned environment; no compulsory cross-environment transfer is evident. If shared IDs are intended to represent physically pooled streams, their mixing and compatibility must be specified