g-blocks-found-in-bennu-samples)\n  62. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2025NatAs...9..199G)\n  63. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-024-02472-9)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.87\n- Feedback: This is a connected, chemically credible conditional-feedstock network with sufficient molecule and reaction representation, consistent IDs and multiple valid routes to glycine. The deterministic conservation check is accepted in full: 15 reactions are balanced with unit coefficients, nine require coefficients, and none violates atom or charge conservation. The inferred coefficients should be encoded for quantitative modeling, but they are not conservation defects. All 24 reactions are assessed above; competing sinks are included with their associated pathways rather than counted as productive sequence steps. Explicit reductant consumption, glycolate competition and secondary glycine consumption strengthen the chemical accounting. Declared boundary feedstocks, neutral acid-base pool notation, representative formation locations and idealized mineral endmember balances are accepted without requiring additional upstream synthesis. Neither pathway multiplicity nor the choice of feedstock framework affects the score. Literature alignment is strong at the reaction-family level, but reaction entries lack individual citations and quantitative operating conditions. Surface, Hydrothermal and Biochemical are insufficient by themselves to verify pH, temperature, pressure, residence time, catalyst state or required material transfers. The principal unresolved issues are hydrolysis kinetics, hydantoin-specific evidence, the initial mixed-valence iron inventory, catalyst specification and product availability after adsorption. Shared molecule identities do not require incompatible conditions to coexist. Problematic reaction flags identify these specification or evidence gaps, not mass-balance failures. The network is not approved under the overall threshold of 0.90, although its pathways meet the separately requested 0.80 pathway threshold. It is a sound basis for a more fully specified synthesis network, rather than a demonstrated environmentally executable system.\n- Issues: []\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.87\n- Feedback: This is a credible, connected glycine-synthesis network at its declared feedstock boundary, but it is not approved at the 0.90 network threshold. All 25 molecule nodes participate, IDs are used consistently in the reaction list, and complete productive routes reach mol_015. Accepting the deterministic audit as instructed, all 22 reaction sets are balanced or balanceable and none is classified as a conservation violation. The mol_020 formula/structure discrepancy requires metadata repair, not rejection of rxn_018. Neutral acid-base pools, mineral endmember equivalents and the initial ferric boundary inventory are reasonable declared abstractions. The supplied literature supports the principal chemistry, and the network appropriately acknowledges upstream supply limitations, competing glycolate production, glycine consumption, passivation and mineral binding. No penalty is assigned for the selected feedstocks, shared mechanisms, pathway count or environment count. The main limitation is operational specification: despite the strategy referring to experimental anchors and screening windows, the submitted reaction entries contain no numerical conditions, reaction-level citations or defined physical habitats beyond broad labels. In particular, Biochemical does not specify a geochemical environment. Hydrolysis kinetics, hydantoin release conditions, active catalyst availability and hydrogen confinement therefore remain insufficiently established. Each productive route can operate within its assigned environment; no compulsory cross-environment transfer is evident. If shared IDs are intended to represent physically pooled streams, their mixing and compatibility must be specified, including possible cyanide interference with metal catalysts. The network demonstrates plausible routes to glycine identity, not a quantified integrated production flux or dissolved product supply. Reaction-score maps include associated competing reactions so that all 22 reactions are assessed; problematic_reactions flags unresolved conditions or evidence gaps, not necessarily invalid chemistry.\n- Issues: []\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glycine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct ten operationally distinct pathway variants around two experimentally supported chemical cores: aminonitrile hydrolysis and reductive amination of glyoxylate. The variants differ in precursor supply, nitrogen source, reductant, mineral interface, or thermal regime; they are not ten unrelated elementary mechanisms. The network contains 25 chemical-species nodes and 22 reaction nodes so that intermediates, competing glycolate formation, reductant consumption, and secondary glycine consumption remain explicit. Water, ammonia, cyanide, formaldehyde, glyoxylate, hydrogen, hydroxylamine, nitrate, and mineral inventories are permitted boundary feedstocks where specified; an efficient geological supply of each is not presumed. In particular, glyoxylate and concentrated cyanide remain upstream bottlenecks. Neutral organic formulas represent acid-base pools, not necessarily the dominant protonation state at the stated pH; explicit hydrons provide charge and redox bookkeeping where needed. Fe(OH)2 and FeOOH represent ferrous and ferric mineral endmember equivalents, not a claim that reacting mixed minerals transform into pure crystalline phases. An initial ferric seed inventory is explicitly required for the mixed-valence branch, without invoking molecular oxygen. Numeric conditions identified as screening windows are proposed tests, not reported kinetic measurements. Shared molecular IDs denote chemical identity, not compulsory mixing: cyanide/copper/sulfide chemistry and metal-catalyzed glyoxylate chemistry occupy separate habitats. Transfers and changes of pH within a pathway are specified below. The single environment_formed value records a representative formation setting; null denotes a supplied boundary species. The schema's intermediate role also covers non-target coproducts. Product formation does not automatically establish dissolved availability, particularly on brucite.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_