ondensation-reduction representation is a mechanistic interpretation rather than an independently quantified elementary pathway. Including it captures a chemically justified sink for both newly formed glycine and the remaining reducing inventory, preventing accumulated glycine from being equated automatically with gross formation."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Dissolved-feedstock surface Strecker pathway", "description": "Supplied formaldehyde, ammonia, and cyanide react in a mildly alkaline pool through transient methanimine and aminoacetonitrile. The aminonitrile-bearing solution then enters a separate hot acidic collection pool for sequential hydrolysis. The required pH change and hydrolysis residence time are explicit bottlenecks; rxn_005 and rxn_011 divert material toward glycolate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Cyanosulfidic cyanohydrin-fed pathway", "description": "HCN photoreduction supplies a formaldehyde precursor pool, which captures additional cyanide as glycolonitrile. Ammonia enrichment after the photochemical stage shifts the cyanohydrin reservoir toward aminoacetonitrile, followed by transfer to the hydrolysis pool. Copper/sulfide speciation, cyanide retention, staged delivery, and glycolate competition remain constraints.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_011", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Reducing-atmosphere discharge-fed Strecker pathway", "description": "Electrical discharge in a locally reducing atmosphere generates HCN and formaldehyde, which are captured into ammonia-containing water and processed by surface Strecker chemistry. A later acidic hydrolysis habitat is required. This differs from P1 in demonstrated precursor-generation chemistry, not in the downstream aminonitrile mechanism, and is not extended to an unmodified neutral atmosphere.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Confined moderate-temperature hydrothermal Strecker pathway", "description": "Supplied formaldehyde, cyanide, and ammonium undergo condensation and hydrolysis in the same approximately 423 K pressurized porewater habitat. The two reaction nodes partition a coupled experiment rather than demanding aminonitrile isolation or separate 25-54 h holds. Cooling after the hot stage is required for product preservation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Dissolved Fe(II) glyoxylate reductive amination", "description": "Supplied glyoxylate and ammonia generate a transient imine that is reduced by dissolved Fe(II), with ferric hydrolysis explicitly included. This is a low-yield, condition-sensitive pathway rather than an iron-catalytic cycle. The proposed near-neutral operating window, buffer demand, and rate must be verified.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Mixed-valence iron-hydroxide glyoxylate amination", "description": "Glyoxylate and ammonia react at an abiotic mixed-valence mineral interface, where structural Fe(II) reduces the imine. The initially ferric mineral fraction is supplied explicitly. Glycolate formation competes for substrate and reducing equivalents; no assumption of continually regenerated Fe(II) is made.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_012", "rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Ammonium-fed ferroan-brucite pathway", "description": "Ammonia-derived glycine imine is reduced by structural Fe(II) in ferroan brucite over a weeks-long residence. The iron inventory, rather than glyoxylate concentration alone, limits production. Glycolate formation, secondary iminodiacetate formation, and possible mineral association reduce recoverable glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_012", "rxn_015"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Nitrate-fed ferroan-brucite pathway", "description": "A coupled mineral reaction supplies reduced nitrogen from nitrate and forms glycine from glyoxylate. This has a different nitrogen source and a ten-electron requirement per glycine, compared with the two-electron ammonia-fed branch. The experimentally supported net step is retained without inventing resolved nitrogen intermediates. Most measured product may require acid extraction from the mineral.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Hydrogen-supported nickel reductive amination", "description": "Glyoxylate and low-to-moderate ammonium inventories form an imine in the same sealed H2-bearing aqueous habitat where supported Ni hydrogenates it. H2 confinement, active metallic surface availability, and competition from direct glycolate formation are essential. The laboratory-supported catalyst is not assumed to be interchangeable with an untested natural mineral.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_012", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Hydroxylamine/native-iron oxime pathway", "description": "Supplied hydroxylamine condenses with glyoxylate, and native iron reduces and deoxygenates the resulting oxime to glycine. The overall hydroxylamine/iron chemistry is supported experimentally; the explicit oxime partition is a mechanistic model requiring verification. Hydroxylamine supply, proton buffering, metal corrosion, and passivation remain constraints.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_020", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_022"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_018", "mol_019", "mol_020"], "convergenc