eded to establish effective nitrile and amide hydrolysis.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify aminonitrile-formation conditions, including pH and ammonia-to-cyanide ratio, and separately document experimentally supported hydrolysis conditions.\",\n        \"Provide the physical basis for any pH or temperature transition and quantify glycine formation relative to competing glycolate formation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.94\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.86,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.88,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.65,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.92,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.8,\n      \"feedback\": \"Patel et al. (2015) supports the cyanosulfidic precursor chemistry and glycolonitrile-to-aminoacetonitrile sequence. However, rxn_002 is a lumped photoreduction/hydrolysis transformation, not evidence for selective accumulation of free formaldehyde. Copper chemistry is acknowledged globally, but its loading, speciation, irradiation conditions and sequential processing are not supplied. Reaction rxn_006 is plausible as a net equilibrium-mediated conversion, not direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group. Reaction rxn_011 is scored as a competing branch, not an additional target-forming step.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify UV spectrum and exposure, copper speciation, sulfide loading, cyanide concentration, buffer and irradiation time; characterize sulfur products rather than assuming S8 is the sole observed product.\",\n        \"Document transfer into ammoniation and hydrolysis conditions, including intermediate retention, additional cyanide availability for rxn_005 and competition from rxn_011.\",\n        \"Link each operational stage to the relevant experimental procedure and distinguish demonstrated conditions from proposed screening windows.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.97,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.9,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.92,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.93,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n      \"feedback\": \"Reducing-gas discharge production followed by aqueous Strecker chemistry is experimentally supported, including by Johnson et al. (2008). Reaction rxn_001 is acceptable as a coarse-grained precursor-production reaction, but its balance does not establish product ratios, selectivity or capture efficiency in a complex discharge mixture. The explicitly reducing feed is accepted without assuming that it represents the global early atmosphere. Electrical input, gas composition, aqueous capture and subsequent hydrolysis conditions remain unspecified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Define discharge energy, gas ratios, pressure, exposure time and aqueous-trap conditions, using measured precursor yields rather than the balanced net equation as a yield model.\",\n        \"Specify how the captured mixture reaches aminonitrile-formation and hydrolysis conditions without excessive precursor loss or destructive exposure.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.92\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.93,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.86,\n      \"feedback\": \"The two net transformations form a valid hydrothermal Strecker route. Hennet et al. (1992), as summarized by Andersson and Holm (2000), provides a relevant benchmark near 150 degrees Celsius and 10 bar. Those are literature conditions, not operating parameters actually specified in this network. Confinement is stated qualitatively, but pressure, residence time, pH and cooling are absent. No unsupported assumption of prolonged exposure above 200 degrees Celsius is required or attributed to this pathway.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify temperature, pressure sufficient to retain the aqueous phase and volatile reagents, pH, concentrations and residence time.\",\n        \"Document product withdrawal or cooling and distinguish the cited mineral redox buffer from a demonstrated Strecker catalyst.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.83\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.9,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.71,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.88,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.81,\n      \"feedback\": \"Paczelt et al. (2023) supports FeSO4-associated glycine formation from glycine-imine chemistry, but the reported approximately 2\u20133% NMR yield and rapid imine hydrolysis caution against assuming efficient conversion. Reaction rxn_013 has an acceptable formal electron and proton budget under the supplied balance check. Its practical performance depends on dissolved Fe(II), ferric precipitation, buffering and rapid interception of the transient imine. These controls are not established by the 'Biochemical' environment label.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify pH, buffer capacity, temperature, ammonia concentration, Fe(II) loading and reaction time, and track dissolved versus precipitated iron.\",\n        \"Test in situ imine formation and reduction together; quantify glycine against imine hydrolysis and direct glyoxylate reduction.\",\n        \"Account for two Fe(II) equivalents per imine reduction and the associated acid-base demand without treating bookkeeping hydrons as literal free-proton concentrations.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.93\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.93,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.75,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.93,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.87,\n      \"feedback\": \"Barge et al. (2020), rather than the 2019 pyruvate study, directly supports glyoxylate-to-glycine chemistry on mixed-valence iron hydroxides. The explicit ferric seed avoids an unsupported oxygen requirement or startup assumption. The mineral-endmember representation is reasonable bookkeeping, although it does not identify the actual reactive phase or surface. Reaction rxn_019 is scored as the experimentally relevant competing glycolate branch. Mineral composition and operating conditions remain insufficiently specified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the mixed-valence mineral phase, initial Fe(II)/Fe(III) ratio, accessible reducing capacity, ammonia concentration, pH, temperature and incubation time.\",\n        \"Use Barge et al. (2020) conditions as explicit benchmarks and measure glycine/glycolate selectivity as mineral oxidation proceeds.\",\n        \"Treat glycine imine as an in situ equilibrium intermediate rather than requiring its isolation or transfer.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_022\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.93,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.75,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.93,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.87,\n      \"feedback\": \"Chimiak et al. (2024) directly supports ammonium-fed glycine formation on ferroan brucite. Reactions rxn_019 and rxn_022 appropriately represent competing glycolate formation and secondary glycine consumption; both are scored as side branches. The Fe(OH)2/FeOOH bookkeeping is acceptable under the stated endmember convention and does not require pure-phase mineral conversion. Actual brucite composition, loading and incubation conditions are missing. Mineral-bound glycine still achieves the molecular target, although it does not establish dissolved availability.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify ferroan-brucite composition, accessible Fe(II) inventory, surface area, pH, temperature, reagent concentrations and incubation time.\",\n        \"Allocate the finite Fe(II) inventory among glycine, glycolate and iminodiacetate formation, and state the normalization basis for any reported yield.\",\n        \"Measure dissolved and mineral-associated glycine separately and define a release process only if downstream dissolved product is required.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_022\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.88,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.9,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.91,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.83,\n      \"feedback\": \"Chimiak et al. (2024) supports the overall nitrate-fed transformation, so rxn_016 is credible as a lumped reaction rather than a proposed elementary mechanism. Its requirement for ten Fe(II) equivalents is chemically meaningful: eight electrons reduce nitrate nitrogen to amine nitrogen and two reduce the carbonyl-derived carbon. Low reported yield and substantial mineral association limit productivity and availability claims, not the identification of glycine as a product. Reactions rxn_019 and rxn_022 are competing branches. Operating conditions and nitrogen-species evolution are not specified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Provide mineral loading, nitrate and glyoxylate concentrations, pH, temperature and incubation time, with an explicit ten-electron reducing-capacity budget per glycine.\",\n        \"Distinguish experimental support for the overall transformation from uncertainty about intermediate nitrogen species and surface mechanisms.\",\n        \"Quantify total and dissolved glycine separately, alongside nitrogen-containing by-products and competing consumption of Fe(II).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.94\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.92,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.69,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.94,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n      \"feedback\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) provides strong experimental support for H2-driven reductive amination over supported nickel, with competing hydroxy-acid production. Reaction rxn_018 correctly captures this competition and is scored as a side branch. The chemistry is sound, but the network does not specify nickel oxidation state, support, loading, hydrogen pressure or gas-liquid transport. A synthetic supported catalyst is not automatically equivalent to a natural nickel-bearing mineral. The declared separation from cyanide/sulfide chemistry avoids compulsory catalyst poisoning by those other branches.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_018\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify catalyst composition and active state, loading, H2 pressure, pH, temperature, ammonia concentration and reaction time; the reported 22 degrees Celsius, pH 8 and 5 bar H2 are useful benchmarks.\",\n        \"Justify the catalyst's presence in the stated habitat or explicitly classify the pathway as a laboratory analogue requiring validation with the proposed natural material.\",\n        \"Measure glycine/glycolate selectivity and catalyst deactivation under the actual feed composition.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.84\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.87,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.9,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.69,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.89,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.81,\n      \"feedback\": \"Oxime formation followed by reduction and N\u2013O bond cleavage is chemically plausible, and Muchowska et al. (2019) supports amino-acid formation using hydroxylamine and metallic iron. That evidence supports the overall reagent combination more directly than it establishes the exact isolated-oxime sequence depicted here. Reaction rxn_021 has a valid four-electron reduction budget under the supplied check. Native-iron corrosion, passivation and competing hydroxylamine reduction remain relevant, but the actual pH, surface state and timing are unspecified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_021\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify iron surface preparation and loading, actual pH and proton donors, temperature, concentrations and residence time.\",\n        \"Confirm the oxime intermediate and glycine product experimentally, distinguishing the proposed intermediate sequence from evidence for the overall reaction.\",\n        \"Quantify iron consumption, hydrogen evolution, ammonia formation and residual hydroxylamine to establish productive reducing efficiency.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"The network is structurally valid and chemically well motivated. All 25 molecule nodes and 22 reaction nodes participate in a connected graph, molecule IDs are consistent, and the supplied deterministic check is accepted as establishing zero conservation violations. The S8 normalization and ten Fe(II) equivalents in rxn_016 are not intrinsically implausible coefficients. Declared boundary feedstocks, ferric seed inventory and mineral-endmember conventions are accepted; no score adjustment is made for feedstock preference, pathway count, shared intermediates or environment order. Both principal reaction families provide credible routes to glycine, and the competing glycolate and iminodiacetate branches improve the accounting of selectivity and product consumption. Side reactions included in pathway reaction_scores are not additional target-forming steps. The central weakness is incomplete operational specification: despite the strategy's references to screening windows and transfer details, the submitted reaction records contain no numerical condition sets or concrete within-pathway transfer protocols. 'Biochemical' alone does not define a physical habitat. Literature alignment is strong at the transformation level, but reaction-level citations and procedure mappings are absent from the network itself. Flagged reactions therefore indicate operational or mechanistic uncertainties, not conservation failures. Glycine formation is credible under suitable conditions; sustained productivity and dissolved availability are not established. The network is not approved at the required overall confidence threshold of 0.90, although its individual pathways meet the separate 0.80 pathway threshold.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_003": 0.92, "rxn_004": 0.97, "rxn_007": 0.92, "rxn_008": 0.91}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.94, "pathway_coherence": 0.95, "environmental_progression": 0.76, "target_achievement": 0.94, "overall_confidence": 0.89, "feedback": "The inferred sequence is a well-supported Strecker route with correctly identified aminonitrile and amide intermediates. The supplied Strecker literature supports glycine formation and competition with cyanohydrin chemistry. Methanimine should represent a transient equilibrium intermediate rather than an accumulated stock. The principal limitation is operational: the reaction records specify only 'Surface', without the pH, temperature, concentrations or residence times needed to establish effective nitrile and amide hydrolysis.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "suggestions": ["Specify aminonitrile-formation conditions, including pH and ammonia-to-cyanide ratio, and separately document experimentally supported hydrolysis conditions.", "Provide the physical basis for any pH or temperature transition and quantify glycine formation relative to competing glycolate formation."]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_002": 0.8, "rxn_005": 0.96, "rxn_006": 0.88, "rxn_007": 0.92, "rxn_008": 0.91, "rxn_011": 0.94}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.86, "pathway_coherence": 0.88, "environmental_progression": 0.65, "target_achievement": 0.92, "overall_confidence": 0.8, "feedback": "Patel et al. (2015) supports the cyanosulfidic precursor chemistry and glycolonitrile-to-aminoacetonitrile sequence. Howev